<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yury of Zvenigorod</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1374–<a href="/years/1434">1434</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Yury Dmitrievich</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yury of Zvenigorod was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-donskoy">Dmitry Donskoy</a> and prince of Zvenigorod and Galich. When his brother Vasily I died in 1425, he claimed the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> against his nephew Vasily II under the old rule of succession from brother to brother, opening a long civil war. He twice seized Moscow, in 1433 and 1434, and died there as grand prince in 1434.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yury-of-zvenigorod.jpg" alt="Yury of Zvenigorod" width="320" height="661" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous Russian painter (1670s-1917) Public domain image (according to PD-Russia-expired) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frescos_in_Cathedral_of_the_Archangel_in_Moscow_-_west_wall_01_-_Yuri_of_Zvenigorod.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
